WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.403
Two years ago, in 2016, we talked about a
paper that enabled us to sit in front of a

00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:12.010
camera, and transfer our gestures onto a virtual
actor.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:17.420
This work went by the name Face2face and showcased
a bunch of mesmerizing results containing

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.550
reenactments of famous political figureheads.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.039
It was quite amazing.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:24.420
But it is nothing compared to this one.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.560
And the reason for this is that the original
Face2face paper only transferred expressions,

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.870
but this new work is capable of transferring
head and torso movements as well.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:39.180
Not only that, but mouth interiors also appear
more realistic and more gaze directions are

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:40.500
also supported.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.180
You see in the comparisons here that the original
method disregarded many of these features

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:47.730
and how much more convincing this new one
is.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:52.710
This extended technique opens up the door
to several, really cool new applications.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.030
For instance, consider this self-reenactment
application.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.370
This means that you can re-enact yourself.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.560
Now, what would that be useful for, you may
ask?

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.140
Well, of course, you can appear to be the
most professional person during a virtual

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.689
meeting, even when sitting at home in your
undergarment.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:13.719
Or, you can quickly switch teams based on
who is winning the game.

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:18.939
Avatar digitization is also possible, this
basically means that we can create a stylized

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:21.930
version of our likeness to be used in a video
game.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:27.130
Somewhat similar to the Memoji presented in
Apple's latest keynote with the iPhone X.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:32.060
And the entire process takes place in real
time without using neural networks.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.900
This is as good as it gets.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.369
What a time to be alive!

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:39.750
Of course, like every other technique, this
one also has its own set of limitations.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.780
For instance, illumination changes in the
environment are not always taken into account,

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.569
and long-haired subjects with extreme motion
may cause artifacts to appear.

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:52.130
In short, don't use this for rock concerts.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.840
And with this, we are also one step closer
to full character re-enactment for movies,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.560
video games and telepresence applications.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.990
This is still a new piece of technology, and
may offer many more applications that we haven't

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:04.990
thought of yet.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.940
After all, when the internet was invented,
who thought that it could be used to order

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.320
pizza or transfer Bitcoin?

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.770
Or order pizza and pay with bitcoin.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.940
Anyway, if you have some more applications
in mind, let me know in the comments section.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

